WEBVTT


00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.696
Tanda Fae and the First Tooth Fee.

00:00:03.696 --> 00:00:10.008
Long before Tanda had wings, she lived beside a cold northern sea.

00:00:10.008 --> 00:00:15.912
Her father built ships from oak, patience, and the sounds hidden in wood.

00:00:15.912 --> 00:00:22.872
When sticky pitch caught her sleeve, Tanda said, “It likes me.”

00:00:22.872 --> 00:00:27.792
That evening, one small tooth wobbled like a loose peg.

00:00:27.792 --> 00:00:31.896
She bit the dried fish to prove it was fine.

00:00:31.896 --> 00:00:37.872
The tooth landed in her palm, small, white, and rather rude.

00:00:37.872 --> 00:00:43.176
Her father washed it carefully, as if it were already precious.

00:00:43.176 --> 00:00:47.832
Then he placed a bright coin beside it. “A Tooth Fee.”

00:00:47.832 --> 00:00:55.392
Tanda covered the tooth. “If I take the coin, is it still mine?”

00:00:55.392 --> 00:01:01.056
“Always. A fee is not a purchase. It is a promise to notice.”

00:01:01.056 --> 00:01:06.048
Together, they threaded it onto a cord near his heart.

00:01:06.048 --> 00:01:11.880
At the dock, he asked again, “May I carry your tooth until I return?”

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:17.280
The shipyard goat studied the new necklace with hungry interest.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:23.184
The goat tried to eat the promise immediately.
Tanda stopped it immediately.

00:01:23.184 --> 00:01:35.352
With winter coming and the village stores running low, Father had to sail far for grain, wool, and timber. Until I come home, he promised.

00:01:35.352 --> 00:01:39.744
The sail rose, and the sea carried him away.

00:01:39.744 --> 00:01:45.000
Tanda watched until the ship became a dark stitch on the horizon.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.544
Across the water came wind, fog, and a hard winter.

00:01:50.544 --> 00:01:59.472
For weeks they crossed cold water, while food barrels emptied and every distant shore felt the same hard winter.

00:01:59.472 --> 00:02:04.440
One morning, the crew found a boy hiding in a barrel.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:09.120
He clutched their rope as if it were the last thing in the world.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:14.520
Tanda's father was tired, enormous, and ready to shout.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:19.296
Then the ordinary tooth tapped softly against his chest.

00:02:19.296 --> 00:02:25.296
He remembered Tanda closing both hands around it. Still mine.

00:02:25.296 --> 00:02:28.920
Her father knelt. “What is the rope for?”

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:32.928
The boy pointed toward the rocks below the harbor.

00:02:32.928 --> 00:02:37.872
A cracked boat waited there beside his shivering little sister.

00:02:37.872 --> 00:02:43.272
Tanda's father brought tools, wool socks, and patience.

00:02:43.272 --> 00:02:47.160
He repaired the boat while the children watched.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:52.152
By morning, both shores were trading help instead of taking it.

00:02:52.152 --> 00:02:56.280
Near his heart, Tanda's tooth grew warm.

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:04.200
Inside the tooth, the moment waited: the story of a child Father had chosen to hear.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:09.096
When the ship returned, Tanda was already running.

00:03:09.096 --> 00:03:15.696
She ran so fast she nearly forgot she had legs—and straight into Father’s arms.

00:03:15.696 --> 00:03:22.224
When he knelt beside her, the tooth was still there—safe against his heart.

00:03:22.224 --> 00:03:26.232
“You brought it back.” “I promised.”

00:03:26.232 --> 00:03:30.456
When Tanda touched it, she smelled herring.

00:03:30.456 --> 00:03:37.128
She saw the two children holding the same rope, safe beside their repaired boat.

00:03:37.128 --> 00:03:41.496
She saw the frightened boy relax when nobody shouted.

00:03:41.496 --> 00:03:46.320
Tanda opened her wooden box and found a clean page.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:52.200
She drew the ship, the rope, two children, and Dad listening.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:57.216
The memory gathered around her drawing and sealed it in gold.

00:03:57.216 --> 00:04:03.888
The box answered with a rooftop, a tiny door, a feather, and a seed.

00:04:03.888 --> 00:04:08.160
One promise had found many possible paths.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:17.016
As Tanda listened, the hidden stories gathered into paths around her, and light formed at her shoulders.

00:04:17.016 --> 00:04:22.896
Then the promise made her small enough to carry a child's memory safely.

00:04:22.896 --> 00:04:29.712
Tanda grew older, learning that every tooth could hold a story worth hearing.

00:04:29.712 --> 00:04:37.032
Beside each sleeping child, she listened for the memories that deserved to be protected.

00:04:37.032 --> 00:04:45.408
Over the years, Tanda became the first Tooth Fairy—the first Keeper of children’s hidden stories.

00:04:45.408 --> 00:04:54.096
She taught other fairies to listen first, and together they built a Network of many traditions joined by one promise.

00:04:54.096 --> 00:05:02.544
The way of keeping changed—from a wooden box to a protected digital memory—but the promise stayed the same.

00:05:02.544 --> 00:05:11.400
A child’s drawing, their words, and the story behind the tooth could stay together until the day they chose to open it.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:15.400
Make a Toothlight
